<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inti Raymi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Festival of the Sun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti">Inti</a> Raymi, the Festival of the Sun, was the main religious festival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>, held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> at the June solstice in honour of the sun god Inti. Banned by the Spanish authorities in the 16th century, it was revived as a public spectacle in 1944 and is now staged each June at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman">Sacsayhuamán</a> above Cusco.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inti-raymi.jpg" alt="Inti Raymi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cyntia Motta · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inti_Raymi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Inti Raymi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> for festival of Inti, honoured Inti, the sun and the most venerated deity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> religion. It marked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a>, the shortest day of the year in the southern hemisphere, and the Inca New Year, when the hours of light begin to lengthen again. According to the chronicler Garcilaso de la Vega, who calls it the most important festival of the empire, Sapa Inca Pachacuti created it. It took place in the Haukaypata, the main square of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>.</p><p>The ceremony lasted nine days, with dances, processions and animal sacrifices to thank <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachamama">Pachamama</a> and ensure a good harvest. The last Inti Raymi attended by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> emperor was held in 1535, after which Spanish colonists and Catholic priests banned it with other Inca religious practices.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In 1944 a historical reconstruction was directed by Faustino Espinoza Navarro with indigenous actors, based largely on the chronicles of Garcilaso de la Vega and covering only the religious ceremony. Since then a yearly theatrical staging has taken place at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman">Saksaywaman</a>, two kilometres from the original site, drawing thousands of visitors.</p><p>Over 800 performers take part in three settings. The ceremony begins at the temple of Qorikancha, historically the main site of sun worship. In the Plaza de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arma-people">Armas</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> meets the city's mayor and exchanges the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quipu">khipu</a> that stands for munay (to want), yachay (to know) and llankay (to work). The central ceremony at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman">Sacsayhuamán</a> includes a report from the four suyus, rites of chicha, sacred fire and holy bread, a llama sacrifice with omens, and dances.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Inti Raymi is still observed in indigenous cultures throughout the Andes, with music, colourful costumes, among them the woven Aya Huma mask, and shared food. In many places it has been connected with the Catholic festival of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> on 24 June, a few days after the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Inti Raymi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Accounts by Pedro Cieza de León, Juan de Betanzos, Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala and Garcilaso de la Vega, whose Comentarios Reales de los Incas appeared in 1609, made the Incas known in Europe, and <b>Inti Raymi</b> is still celebrated at Cusco.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Inti Raymi</b>, the Festival of the Sun, was the main religious festival of the Inca Empire, held in Cusco at the June solstice, the Andean midwinter, in honour of Inti, the sun god and the divine ancestor claimed by the Sapa Inca.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote>Priests consulted the shrines, made offerings of llamas, cloth, coca and maize beer, and conducted the festivals of the calendar, above all <b>Inti Raymi</b> at the June solstice.</blockquote><a href="/societies/inca-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Inca Empire</a></li></ul></section>
